michael: FYI - onboarding sample hit a nasty infinite auth/refresh loop on a Pixel 6 running Android 12. App logs show repeated 401 -> Authenticator -> retry -> 401 forever until client OOMs.

michael: Repro: POST /upload returns 307 -> proxy-> server redirects to /login -> OkHttp Authenticator triggers refresh, but refresh call itself uses the same OkHttp client. Injector races and we end up recursively calling authenticate.

michael: Stack is basically repeated RealCall.execute -> Authenticator.authenticate -> RealCall.execute -> ... not a single clear exception, just repeats until we hit retries limits.

tara: This is a classic anti-pattern: Authenticator should not synchronously use the same client (with authenticator set) to refresh tokens. That causes re-entry. Use a dedicated client without an Authenticator for token refresh.

ivan: Also check redirect handling: OkHttp will follow 307/308 for POST in some configs and replay body. If auth refresh mutates headers, a redirect back to auth endpoint can retrigger Authenticator. Try disabling followRedirects for the client used for auth-relevant flows: `.followRedirects(false).followSslRedirects(false)`.

priya: Quick workaround we applied in the sample: make the Authenticator call a tokenProvider that uses a tiny client:```java
OkHttpClient tokenClient = new OkHttpClient.Builder()
  .authenticator(Authenticator.NONE)
  .retryOnConnectionFailure(false)
  .build();

class MyAuthenticator implements Authenticator {
  @Override
  public Request authenticate(Route route, Response response) throws IOException {
    synchronized (tokenLock) {
      if (tokenLooksFresh()) return null; // another thread refreshed
      Token t = tokenClient.newCall(refreshReq).execute().body().string();
      return response.request().newBuilder().header("Authorization", "Bearer " + t).build();
    }
  }
}
```

priya: Key bits: separate client, retryOnConnectionFailure=false for refresh, and small synchronized section so only one thread does refresh. Otherwise multiple concurrent 401s can trigger many refreshes.

ivan: Another subtlety: Retrofit suspend adapter + enqueue behave differently for Authenticator callbacks. In suspend path the adapter may create and cancel Calls differently which can interact with authenticators when redirects happen mid-body. We added an integration test for both suspend and enqueue paths.

michael: We also saw that gzipped request bodies combined with 307 redirects caused replay behavior that made things worse (server redirected then client resent gzipped body and server treated it as new). For onboarding samples we now avoid streaming gzipped bodies for auth-sensitive flows.

tara: Document these in the onboarding guide: 1) never call the same client from inside Authenticator, 2) prefer a separate token client, 3) set retryOnConnectionFailure=false for token refresh clients, 4) guard refresh with a single-refresh mutex or CompletableDeferred pattern for coroutines.

ivan: For SDK parity note: Java synchronous clients historically hid some of these re-entry issues; Kotlin coroutines + suspend adapters surface them because of different queuing. Add a compatibility test matrix: emulator vs physical devices (Pixel6/7) and both call paths.

priya: CI: created a tiny repro and device job: https://ci.internal/repro/1729999999-auth-reentrancy-1 (includes APK, minimal server that issues 307->/login, and python proxy).

michael: I'm pushing a patch to the sample that: 1) replaces in-authenticator refresh calls with a tokenProvider hook that uses a separate OkHttpClient, 2) adds a runtime warning if an Authenticator tries to use the default client, 3) updates docs with example code and a section on redirects + gzipped bodies.

tara: One more try: in long term we should recommend SDK helpers: expose a built-in token refresher utility in our mobile SDK so users don't implement brittle Authenticators. It can provide the safe client and the single-flight semantics.

